\\[\\frac{r_1^2}{r_1 + 1} + \\frac{r_2^2}{r_2 + 1} + \\dots + \\frac{r_{98}^2}{r_{98} + 1}.\\]","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"4a798512a6002ac0f54c431dff14185f63a9537562e818a5fc23f19aec39731d"},"seconds":62.27854681015015,"seed":97009431170652465,"stop":"eos","text":"To solve the problem, we start by recognizing the polynomial given:\n\\[x^{98} + x^{97} + x^{96} + \\dots + x^2 + x + 1 = 0.\\]\nThis polynomial can be rewritten using the formula for the sum of a geometric series:\n\\[\\frac{x^{99} - 1}{x - 1} = 0,\\]\nfor \\(x \\neq 1\\). The roots of the polynomial are the 99th roots of unity, excluding 1. Let's denote the 99th roots of unity by \\(\\omega^k\\) for \\(k = 0, 1, 2, \\ldots, 98\\), where \\(\\omega = e^{2\\pi i / 99}\\). The roots of the polynomial are \\(\\omega, \\omega^2, \\ldots, \\omega^{98}\\).\n\nWe need to find the value of the sum:\n\\[\\sum_{k=1}^{98} \\frac{\\omega^{2k}}{\\omega^k + 1}.\\]\n\nTo simplify \\(\\frac{\\omega^{2k}}{\\omega^k + 1}\\), we can rewrite it as:\n\\[\\frac{\\omega^{2k}}{\\omega^k + 1} = \\frac{\\omega^{2k} + 1 - 1}{\\omega^k + 1} = \\frac{\\omega^{2k} + 1}{\\omega^k + 1} - \\frac{1}{\\omega^k + 1} = \\omega^k - \\frac{1}{\\omega^k + 1}.\\]\n\nThus, the sum we need to evaluate becomes:\n\\[\\sum_{k=1}^{98} \\left( \\omega^k - \\frac{1}{\\omega^k + 1} \\right) = \\sum_{k=1}^{98} \\omega^k - \\sum_{k=1}^{98} \\frac{1}{\\omega^k + 1}.\\]\n\nThe sum of all the 99th roots of unity, excluding 1, is:\n\\[\\sum_{k=1}^{98} \\omega^k = -1,\\]\nbecause the sum of all 99th roots of unity is 0.\n\nNext, we need to evaluate \\(\\sum_{k=1}^{98} \\frac{1}{\\omega^k + 1}\\). Consider the polynomial \\(P(x) = x^{98} + x^{97} + \\cdots + x + 1\\). The roots of \\(P(x)\\) are \\(\\omega, \\omega^2, \\ldots, \\omega^{98}\\). If we substitute \\(x = -1\\) in the polynomial \\(P(x+1)\\), we get:\n\\[P(x+1) = (x+1)^{98} + (x+1)^{97} + \\cdots + (x+1) + 1.\\]\nThe roots of \\(P(x+1)\\) are \\(\\omega - 1, \\omega^2 - 1, \\ldots, \\omega^{98} - 1\\). The sum of the reciprocals of the roots of \\(P(x+1)\\) is the negative of the coefficient of \\(x^{97}\\) divided by the constant term. The constant term of \\(P(x+1)\\) is \\(P(1) = 99\\), and the coefficient of \\(x^{97}\\) is the sum of the coefficients of \\(x^{98}, x^{97}, \\ldots, x\\) in the expansion of \\((x+1)^{98}, (x+1)^{97}, \\ldots, (x+1)\\), which is 98. Therefore, the sum of the reciprocals of the roots of \\(P(x+1)\\) is:\n\\[-\\frac{98}{99}.\\]\nThus,\n\\[\\sum_{k=1}^{98} \\frac{1}{\\omega^k + 1} = -\\frac{98}{99}.\\]\n\nPutting it all together, we have:\n\\[-1 - \\left(-\\frac{98}{99}\\right) = -1 + \\frac{98}{99} = -\\frac{1}{99}.\\]\n\nTherefore, the final answer is:\n\\[\\boxed{-\\frac{1}{99}}.\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{-\\frac{1}{99}}","correct":false,"format":"boxed","parseable":true}}
